{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1506186100,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDEyNjIzOTQxODA="},"added":["metadata"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1506186115,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDEyNjIzOTQyODQ="},"added":["question"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1507145032,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMzNDI2Mjg5Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1856#issuecomment-334262892"},"message":"I am not sure if I got that right. You want to aggregate the names of fields that cannot have their values aggregated, correct?\n\nBut then you want to have a second parser run that pretty much replaces the metadata and build a new search index?\n\nThis would be the same as to blacklist some fields (now possible in #1630) and to record which fields were removed (not yet possible, because it would need to be done per each regex in the content metadata)....","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1507207084,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMzNDQ1MTg0NA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1856#issuecomment-334451844"},"message":"From what I understand the solution should be to blacklist certain fields for aggregation all together. So you can not get that metadata if you don't have access. However, if you have access you \"prove\" it by installing that dataset and getting those files. IIRC, then a search will include those fields, since the parser is asked for metadata of the now present data (not for aggregation).\nI didn't find the blacklisting yet to have a look whether it works that way, though.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1570266024,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzODYzMTY4Nw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1856#issuecomment-538631687"},"message":"This general idea is now to move towards configurable and group-able metadata extractors, such that their entire output could be directed into a dedicated set of annexed metadata extract files. It would then become the duty of an extractor to guarantee (or not to be able to do that) that something is safe for dissemination. Putting this in the scope of an extractor is more practical, as those can be tailored and deployed for/in a specific context, rather than having to configure each dataset or search index.","files":null}]}